[Music]  hello everyone and welcome to today's  webinar medical laboratory performance  benchmarking current practice and future  perspectives  i am marie stone of lab roots and i'll  be your moderator for today's event  today's educational web seminar is  presented by lab roots and brought to  you by abbott  to learn more visit core laboratory dot  abbott  we encourage you to participate today by  submitting any questions you may have  during the presentation  to do so simply type them into the ask a  question box and click send  we'll answer as many questions as we  have time for at the end of the  presentation  you may also submit any technical issues  here as well if you have trouble seeing  or hearing the presentation  i'd like to now welcome our speaker  dr wolfgang huff medical director vienna  health care group clinic heights in  vienna austria  dr huff you may now begin your  presentation  thank you marie for your kind  introduction and welcome again everyone  i've roughly structured this talk in the  general background a discussion of a  recent study and perspectives for  individual laboratories  first of all  my personal story  why am i such a fan  for benchmarking the short answer is it  helps to make good decisions  the long answer begins with  me being a general practitioner and  regarding laboratory  standards as very high particularly in  the age of evidence-based medicine  then i received training as a laboratory  professional which is  subjectively confirmed my view regarding  standardization in laboratories  but i also found that there is  much room for development  in particular there is a lack of  peer-reviewed data for management  decisions  what is benchmarking how can we define  it  a benchmark is something that serves as  a standard by which others may be  measured or judged according to  merriam-webster  quality  is a relative concept  as it said in medicine today's state of  the art may be tomorrow's grounds for a  trial in a court of law  to assess whether we deliver good  quality we need a reference  in clinical medicine we use guidelines  in the labora laboratory we don't have  much of that in particular regarding  management decisions  this is where benchmarking comes into  play  the laboratory traditionally focuses on  measurement quality  there is a host of external quality  assessments available and widely  established  however  there is also an increasing need to  improve quality in the interaction with  the hospital and management decisions  in particular regarding financial  sustainability  in pubmed the standard database to  research on this there is little to be  found  when you type in medical laboratory and  benchmarking as a search term there are  34 hits  to be found  you can find more on the term eqa  but this is primarily relating to topics  of measurement  right now a lot of international  conferences focus on the so-called  pre-analytical phase this is the phase  where about 65  laboratory errors occur and about 65  percent of costs incursed by the  laboratory  are  generated  so there is a need for data relevant to  management decisions  but  why isn't it here already  as i said benchmarking  either too mostly focused on instrument  and essay performance there are a few  international  studies  trying to compare different laboratories  in different countries different regions  of the world  and they most rely on so-called lis data  laboratory information system data  this is a particularly the case for  the ifcc the international federation  for clinical chemistry working groups on  laboratory errors in patient safety  which extracts  most of its data from  data that is generally to be found in  the laboratory information system there  are some questions that need to be  extracted by hand from different sources  and there are a few studies that focused  on management approaches but  they have not yet published  questionnaires  indeed we didn't find any published  questionnaire regarding  worldwide approaches to  quality and laboratory management so  there need there is a need to generate  and publish a questionnaire  what do we need to do in particular  evidence-informed decision-making is  rapidly becoming the norm  in medicine there is the so-called  european union hope program  hope is in the european hospital and  healthcare federation  2019  the theme was  evidence informed decision making and  healthcare management  and this year's theme is using evidence  in healthcare management so it directly  relates to the 2019 theme  the basic idea is to use reliable data  to support  decision making and management  you cannot  identify the exactly best decision of  course but the more data you have  the better informed your decisions in  general are  so we need to recognize the status quo  in comparison to others  and the academic approach to do this  would be to first design a questionnaire  then  design a reliable data elicitation  process  validate both in the pilot study and  then  use them widely  in particular  we should use the pdca cycle gaming  cycle plan do check act  to continuously improve such a  questionnaire  this idea was the basis for the medical  laboratory benchmarking  that  we publish recently  now why do we need the questionnaire  posing quasi-redundant questions to  elicit reliable information in the end  directly estimating one's own  performance in relation to others is not  the strength the human mind  as you can see  in the study we conducted recently about  98 of laboratories rated their  performance at or above the industry  average this is mathematically  impossible for normally distributed data  now having sketched the general  background i will proceed to talk about  a recent study  and narrow down on the challenges and  opportunities associated with  benchmarking  a benchmarking study relevant to  laboratory management and needs to cover  three key aspects  the process dimension the clinical  dimension and the financial dimension  i will now present a few questions that  were deemed relevant by a group of group  of experts  and  minded this questionnaire has already  been published  you can see the citation at the bottom  of the slide and i can always look it up  in the process dimension three examples  for questions for items actually used  was  does your laboratory measure defined key  performance indicators  examples given very employee  productivity workspace utilization  the amount of implemented auto  validation rules and so on  another example  is  which of the following turnaround times  displayed below  does your laboratory monitor  turnaround times can be defined in  various manners  most of the laboratories  use as their turnaround time the  so-called lab turnaround time  which is defined  from the beginning of sample reception  to the end of recite release  the most um  interesting turnaround time of course is  the so-called total  turnaround time that starts with the  order  and ends with the result release as more  comprehensive approach still is the  brain-to-brain turnaround time that  starts with order and events ends with  physicians access to the data  the third example  was do you use any of advanced id  functionalities listed here is where for  example smartphone related alerts and  applications  instrument capacity monitoring and  information dashboards display kpis  for the clinical dimension of laboratory  benchmarking example questions are which  of the following services does your  laboratory provide to physicians  for example results and interpretations  in combination  proactive consultation complex patient  cases  or diagnostic pathways guidance  how regular did your laboratory and  clinical care teams review the  diagnostic pathways or guidance together  in the last 12 months  or is your laboratory combining data  digitally without diagnostic disciplines  a very interesting item was also the  question regarding  the laboratory measuring impact on  patient outcomes  this is particularly seldomly used  the last but not least dimension is the  financial dimension  most of the laboratories  do not like to give away that direct  financial results  but  for a benchmarking study to be relevant  we need we need to be we need to have  some estimate about the financial  dimension  and items deemed relevant by experts for  what percentage of results are also  validated  um  how many full-time equivalents  were used to operate analytical  workstations  or  the physical space needed  to operate in a difficult workstations  as our goal in designing a study  of this kind is generating  data  that  fulfill the so-called  measurement quality criteria of  objectivity reliability and validity  we trained a host of professionals  to identify valid data in direct  one-on-one conversations  an online questionnaire was filled out  by heads of laboratories  and these trained professionals together  which assured confidentiality and  anonymity  of course there was a possibility to  give contact data to get individual  feedback  the design process of the questionnaire  was iterative  first of all there was an extensive  literature research  and then we used all the experience and  the input for informal focus groups of  laboratory professionals  to reduce  the number of items in the questionnaire  to 50  in order that the questionnaire was  short  and could generate high participation  and high completion rates  as mentioned it was essential to train  professionals to ensure consistency and  confidentiality  with the pseudonymized data  the resulting questionnaire consisted of  the three dimensions operational  performance integrated clinical care  performance and financial sustainability  there were some items regarding general  information at the beginning altogether  50 items  773 laboratories  answered the survey  as far as we know this is the  largest survey to date regarding  laboratory performance  in management  the top 10 participating countries were  italy russia saudi arabia spain united  kingdom germany greece  united arab emirates south africa and  serbia  all together  about  60 percent of laboratories were  government hospital laboratories  but fifth were private commercial  laboratories and about another fifth  private hospital laboratories there were  a few  research laboratories testing  laboratories that were excluded in the  further analysis  now how do laboratories re rate  how do laboratories rate in the three  dimensions  in all three dimensions laboratories  were about normally distributed  which is a prerequisite for the  statistical analysis we performed  afterwards  this is so called factor analysis  which  more or less automatically  sorts the individual items used in the  questionnaire into different  sub-dimensions  in the subdimension of operational  performance  we found seven items in the  sub-dimension of integrated clinical  care performance  six items and in the sub-dimension of  financial sustainability  we also found seven items  you can identify them here  and  this is basically the central figure  of the study the central plot  you can see that there is a correlation  between operation performance and  integrated clinical care performance  financial sustainability is coated in  green amber and red  there is a group of legumes  in the  lower left-hand quadrant  and a group of leaders  in the  top  right hand quadrant  but the interesting thing is the  deviation from this rule of correlation  which might be of interest to individual  laboratories there is for example one  laboratory  which is indicated with the blue arrow  that is in the upper right quadrant  rates very high in operational  performance and rates very high on  integrated care performance but  is in the lower quartile of financial  sustainability  for the individual laboratory it might  be of interest to identify  the things that could be optimized  to increase financial sustainability  and this can be done in the  self-analysis in this case  the laboratory among others  had  practically no autovalidation  in all the laboratory specialties like  clinical chemistry immunoassays  hematology urinalysis coagulation  victoriology root in molecular and  specialized molecular  in this second example  we examine the laboratory that is  above the median  in this case of integrated clinical care  performance and financial sustainability  but there's a laggards  in a sub-dimension of operational  performance  again  [Music]  we can examine the reasons for this  and we would find that  the laboratory doesn't measure patient  satisfaction  doesn't measure clinician satisfaction  and doesn't measure employee  satisfaction  furthermore  it doesn't have  any procedures relating to lean or six  sigma  the third example is a laboratory that  rates relatively high on operational  performance and financial sustainability  but  rates rather low on integrated clinical  care performance  the reason for this is for example that  it doesn't combine any data digitally  with other disciplines  so what can be done in general  for an individual laboratory  i have now  discussed the background of benchmarking  i have discussed this study  but for the individual laboratory we  need some action items the typical  approach  for your lab would be to  identify  the subscale or subscales of interest  after participating in such a  benchmarking study identify the items of  interest  in the subscales of interest  and its relative position to other  laboratories  in the country for example  or  in the specific  laboratory community like hospital  laboratories  or commercial laboratories  and then discuss with staff  and make plans for the coming year  how to improve on these measures  ideally a ptc asia cycle should be  started  the concrete action trajectories are  regarding the location of existing  resources  or  the generation of new resources budget  requests etc  the general benefits  of the benchmarking approach are that  benchmarking allows quantification and  thus comparison  and ideally business case generation  always remember that top management  needs  data to do  investment and location allocation of  resources  and they even might need some arguments  for shareholders  this is the publication to sum up  the background was that there is a  historical lack of global initiatives on  collecting and benchmarking management  data  for a diagnostic  medical laboratories  we did a study that was published in the  pilot  you saw in the slides above  and  found its follow-up study  in  the study published in clinical  chemistry and laboratory medicine that  gathered data from  all around europe middle east and africa  its aims were to establish a good  practice approach towards benchmarking  to generate the snapshot of the current  state of the art in this region  and  most importantly to set the stage for  global follow-up studies  your immediate reaction might be how to  participate in these global follow-up  studies  you can participate using this link here  and for more background information  visit the core laboratory site at evert  this was it from my part  and i'm ready to take questions now  thank you dr hoof for your informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you'd like to ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located at the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  so let's get started our first question  is  how can i be sure that my data are kept  confidential  well um  by default  data questions and processing in this  study are of course anonymized and there  are several regulations like the general  data protection regulation of the  european union  and other relevant regulations that are  natural related to  but  of course there is the opportunity  to get individual feedback if possible  and decide by the participant in this  case contact details can be given  and  data will be returned to to the  participant but data analysis per se is  always done with anonymized data  all right thank you  next question  since data are anonymous can i be sure  that they are representative for the  laboratory landscape  first of all  representativeness is a relative concept  it depends on the subgroup you're  examining  and of course there is some potential  for bias in any study  nevertheless  i already mentioned that  the study presented here is the largest  study to date  so it is probably the best you can do  right now  and  regarding utility which is uh the most  important question for each and every  individual laboratory  it's it's like with the telephone the  more people use it  the more benefit you might have  and  i hope that this webinar will also  increase participation and that's the  utility for you  great thank you  next question  how exactly do you envision benchmarking  to continue in the future  i believe benchmarking  will be  much more central  in the future of laboratory medicine  in particular i think that each  laboratory will probably not only have a  sort of chief benchmarking officer  but also higher degree of benchmarking  literacy  in the entirety of its staff  to this regard we're currently running  the follow-up study the links to which  you can see on the screen  and promoting knowledge about the  utility of benchmarking on an  international scale  all right  and it looks like we have time for one  more question  how has the benchmarking data helped  your lab  well the way benchmarking data helps the  individual lab  depends to a high degree on the current  state of development of this lab  you saw in the scatter plots that  individual laboratories have a  quite a varying degree  of  so to say majority  in our particular lab we felt the need  to increase automation and also focus  more intensely on the pre-analytical  phase in concordance  with the international conferences and  their focus on the pre-analytical phase  of course this is normal most  professionals you most of the time know  the needs of a laboratory but you don't  have the data to prove it  in discussion discussions with the  management  so  the benchmarking study was a wake-up  call call for us all  and we now strive to achieve a fully  digital ordering process as a side note  during the current pandemic  the city of vienna has impressively  demonstrated  a full electronic ordering process  for the masses so  it should be achievable by any  laboratory worldwide  all right thank you dr huff do you have  any final comments for our audience  well i enjoyed the webinar and thank you  for the great questions  have a nice day  thanks thank you again dr huff for your  time today in your important research  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor abbott for underwriting  today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  roots will alert you via email when it's  available for replay we encourage you to  share that email with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  until next time goodbye  you

